{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-220",
  "title": "Node Schema Analysis",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Node Schema Analysis"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You have a solid, well‑thought‑out schema in `graph_schema.md` and Convex is a great fit for this kind of graph‑style data, but there are a few trade‑offs and “gotchas” to plan for. Here’s my take:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Object‑vs‑Table Mapping"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Nodes** → single `nodes` table"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Fields: `path: string`, `type: \"file\"|\"dir\"|\"symlink\"|\"entity\"`, plus a `metadata: { … }` object."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Convex lets you store nested objects, but only top‑level fields are indexed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Edges** → single `edges` table"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Fields: `from: string`, `to: string`, `relation: string`, and optional `metadata` (flatten any subfields you’ll filter on)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Graph** and **GraphIndex** are just in‑memory/virtual (you won’t persist the full graph structure as one document; you’ll rebuild or cache it via queries on `nodes`+`edges`)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Complex Metadata"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Embedding vectors** (e.g. `embedding?: number[]`):"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Convex can hold arrays of numbers, but very large vectors (512–2048 dims) can bloat your documents and slow queries."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Option A (recommended): push embeddings to a vector DB (Pinecone/Weaviate), store only an `embeddingContentHash` or external ID in Convex."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Dynamic Runtime Fields** (e.g. `contextualRelevance`):"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Compute these on the fly in Convex functions (queries/actions), don’t persist them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• Keeps your storage lean and avoids staleness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Indexing & Query Patterns"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Convex auto‑indexes all top‑level scalar fields. If you need to filter by `metadata.userTags` or `metadata.entityType`, consider duplicating those into top‑level columns:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "before → after"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "```"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "// ...existing schema..."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "metadata: { userTags?: string[] }"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "```"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "// …"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "// becomes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "userTags?: string[];"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "metadata: { … };"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "```"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-29",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Edges** queries:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• You’ll often do `rg.table(\"edges\").filter(e => e.from === nodeId)`. That’s fast."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "• If you need “incoming” lookups (`to === nodeId`), you get that for free too."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Performance & Scale"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- For very large graphs (100k+ nodes/edges), you’ll want to page results or maintain lightweight adjacency‐caches."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Convex functions are single‐region, low‑latency, but deep graph traversals (>3 hops) could require multiple function calls. You can batch or write a single function that does breadth‑first expansion in memory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Versioning & Edge Metadata"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- You can store `version_number` or `dependency_level` right on the edge’s `metadata`. Just flatten the fields you’ll query by or sort on."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Next Steps"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Define your tables in `convex/database.ts` (or `schema.ts`) with the mappings above."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Generate Convex’s TypeScript types and scaffold basic CRUD functions (`createNode`, `linkEdge`, `getNeighbors`, etc.)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Decide where embeddings live (external vs. in‑table)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. Plan your “contextualRelevance” algorithm as an on‑demand Convex query."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Overall: **Yes**, Convex can faithfully host your FileGraph data model. The key is flattening/indexing any fields you’ll query on, offloading heavy vectors if needed, and computing dynamic scores at query‑time. Once your tables are defined, you can iterate quickly on functions to explore the graph. Let me know if you want to see a starter `convex/database.ts` draft!"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}